Test and benchmark programs for _lib/xterm.c. Both need /etc/font.bfnt (make -C ../_font install)
and Xvfb. Build with `make`.

xt_test   Renders 6 frames of a random stress grid (glyphs, colours, underline/strike, fullwidth,
          emoji, missing-glyph boxes, cursor, partial redraws) and idles ~1.5s so a screenshot can
          be taken. ./snap.sh [depth] prints one hash per frame. Build it from two versions of
          xterm.c and compare the output: identical hashes = identical pixels.

xt_bench  300 frames, mode in argv[1]:
            a  every cell changes, default colours (like xterm_demo)
            b  every cell changes and has its own colours (worst case for the alpha table)
            c  text-like screen, one row changes per frame (what xtmux mostly does)
            d  text-like screen, every row changes per frame
          ./bench.py MODE [DEPTH] [BIN...] prints min CPU/wall time over several runs, so several
          builds can be compared in one go, e.g.  ./bench.py c 24 ./old_bench ./xt_bench
